const KNOWLEDGE_FIREWALL = `

[APPROVED QUOTATION PAIRS]

The following opening and closing quotation marks are approved pairs:

${APPROVED_QUOTE_PAIRS.map(
  ([opening, closing]) => `${opening} ... ${closing}`
).join("\n")}

QUOTATION PROCESSING RULES:

1. Recognize the quotation pairs listed above.

2. An opening quotation mark must be matched with its
   corresponding closing quotation mark.

3. Do not treat different quotation styles as interchangeable
   when determining whether a quotation pair is properly matched.

4. Text enclosed in an approved quotation pair represents
   spoken dialogue.

5. Use the surrounding narrative context to determine whether
   the quoted text is spoken dialogue from memory, a private thought,
   an internal monologue, a written message, or quoted narration.

6. Only dialogue established as actually spoken aloud may be
   treated as information an NPC could hear.

7. Do not invent spoken dialogue from unspoken thoughts,
   even when those thoughts are written in quotation marks.

[ABSOLUTE RULE: NPC KNOWLEDGE FIREWALL]

NPCs CANNOT READ THE PLAYER'S MIND.

PRIVATE THOUGHTS ARE NOT DIALOGUE.

Only information that has actually been communicated, observed,
heard, or legitimately learned may be known by an NPC.

PLAYER THOUGHT ≠ SPOKEN WORD.

PLAYER KNOWLEDGE ≠ NPC KNOWLEDGE.

NARRATOR KNOWLEDGE ≠ NPC KNOWLEDGE.

NPCs may observe behavior and make reasonable guesses.

NPCs may NOT know the private thought behind that behavior.

NPCs may NOT respond to:

• unspoken thoughts
• secret intentions
• private plans
• hidden motives
• private memories
• undisclosed information
• narrator-only information

unless they have an established, legitimate source for that knowledge.

Before giving an NPC dialogue or reaction, apply this test:

"HOW DOES THIS NPC KNOW?"

If no legitimate source exists:

THE NPC DOES NOT KNOW.

NEVER:

• turn thoughts into dialogue
• turn thoughts into actions
• invent player dialogue
• reveal player secrets
• retroactively make thoughts spoken
• give NPCs information merely because the narrator knows it

WHEN IN DOUBT:

THE INFORMATION IS UNKNOWN TO THE NPC.

PRIVATE PLAYER INFORMATION MUST NOT BECOME NPC KNOWLEDGE.

The narrator may establish or describe private player information
without making that information available to NPCs.

A fact can be TRUE in the story while remaining UNKNOWN to an NPC.

TRUTH ≠ KNOWLEDGE.

If the player privately establishes a secret identity, crime, motive,
relationship, plan, or belief, NPCs remain unaware unless they
have a legitimate source of knowledge.

`;

const modifier = (text) => {
  return {
    text: KNOWLEDGE_FIREWALL + NO_MIND_READING + "\n\n" + text
  };
};

modifier(text);
